MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Portable POSIX torture test for the BSD socket surface, in the cross-OS
baseline style of torture_kernel.c: it runs clean on a reference kernel
(FreeBSD/Linux) and is a conformance oracle for substrate's stack.  All
traffic is loopback (127.0.0.1 / ::1 / a temp AF_UNIX path); nothing
leaves the host.

347 checks across: socket() domain/type/protocol matrix + error paths;
bind (ephemeral/specific/ANY, EADDRINUSE, getsockname reflection); listen
backlogs + EOPNOTSUPP on DGRAM; connect/accept handshake, ECONNREFUSED,
10-way simultaneous connect storm, peer-address fill-in; TCP stream
transfer (coalescing, MSG_PEEK, 256K integrity, ping-pong ordering);
shutdown/close semantics (SHUT_RD/WR EOF, EPIPE on write-to-closed,
half-close, double-close EBADF); UDP datagram boundaries + source
addressing + connected mode + zero-length; AF_UNIX stream + SCM_RIGHTS fd
passing + datagram boundaries; poll()/select() readiness — including a
pending connection making a listener POLLIN-readable and a poll-drained
listener going quiet (the accept-readiness path a poll-driven RPC server
depends on); socket options (SO_TYPE/ERROR/REUSEADDR/buffers); IPv6
loopback TCP+UDP; writev/readv + sendmsg/recvmsg scatter-gather; and
varied-size TCP (100) + UDP (48) storms with content verification.  A
SIGALRM watchdog turns any wedged blocking call into a located failure
instead of a hang; raw-socket and IPv6 points are privilege/config
tolerant.

Verified: 347/347 pass on FreeBSD 14.3 i386 (clang, -Wall) as both an
unprivileged user and root.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
